    {
      "name": "document-model/number-exempt/json-4301-digit-integer-part-is-a-number-and-reads-as-infinity",
      "spec": "docs/02-document-model.md#24-safety-limits",
      "operation": "parse",
      "purpose": "edge-case",
      "input": {
        "format": "json",
        "text": "{\"a\": .5}"
      },
      "expect": {
        "ok": true,
        "document": {
          "edges": [
            [
              "a",
              {
                "scalar": {
                  "kind": "number",
                  "value": "Infinity"
                }
              }
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "comment": "D-28: the digit limit bounds an `integer`, and a literal with a fraction is a `number`, so a JSON literal with 4 301 digits before the point is not refused; it rounds to binary64 and overflows to +Infinity (D-29) with no diagnostic. E-33 spells the non-finite value as a string. Verified live 2026-10-18 at the ports default-branch tips: passes on Python, TypeScript, Rust, Go and Java."
    },
